  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1130 PM CDT Wed Aug 12 2026\n\nLate evening water vapor satellite imagery and RAP upper air \nanalysis reveal weak subtropical ridging centered over the central \nGulf Coast, with southwesterly flow upstream over the central \nplains. Short range ensembles agree the ridge will remain static \ndaytime Thursday as a surface lee trough moves eastward into western \nKS. With the core of this southwest-northeast oriented low \nstraddling I-70, a weak cold front on the low's backside will \ntrickle into our northwest zones, shaving a few degrees off \nafternoon temperatures as highs range from the mid/upper 90s \nnorthwest to 105-110 southeast. A weak upper level disturbance \nis also progged to eject northeast onto the central plains, \nwhich will promote the development of scattered thunderstorms by\nmid/late afternoon along and near the boundary. While deep-\nlayer shear at or below 20 kts will largely limit severity, an \nisolated, marginally severe wind gust or two cannot be ruled \nout, favoring areas along and N of KS-96 where multiple HREF \nmembers show the most pronounced clustering.\n\nLittle to no change is expected in the synoptic pattern daytime \nFriday as the upper ridge holds fast over southern Dixie Alley. The \nprevious day's surface low over west-central KS will begin to \nwashout as 500-mb heights rise, but a convectively reinforced \nboundary that was forced into our southeast zones overnight will \npersist through the afternoon, supporting a more meaningful and \nwidespread break from the heat with highs ranging from the upper 80s \nnorthwest to the upper 90s through an Elkhart-Dodge City-Hays line. \nUnfortunately, the intense heat will continue for our southeast \nzones as highs rise into the 102-106 range once again. Similar to \nThursday, all HREF members are suggesting another round of mid/late \nafternoon thunderstorms is possible in the vicinity of the boundary, \nposing an isolated, marginally severe wind gust threat.\n\nOver the weekend into early next business week, medium range \nensembles continue to suggest the upper ridge over the southeast \nCONUS will jog in place through Sunday before retrograding quickly \non Monday to the Desert Southwest. This synoptic evolution will \nmanifest in a cooling trend Saturday through Monday, followed by a \nwarming trend through the end of the period. Precipitation chances \nwill also decrease substantially after Monday as DNVA ahead of the \nridge suppresses convective activity.",
